HOUSE = ('<svg viewBox="0 0 640 300" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="Arial" font-size="12" role="img" aria-label="Structure of an argumentative essay: introduction with claim, body paragraphs with reasons, counterclaim paragraph, conclusion">'
  '<text x="320" y="20" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold" fill="#0A1F3D" font-size="15">The shape of an argumentative essay</text>'
  '<path d="M120 36 h400 l-40 50 h-320Z" fill="#0A1F3D"/><text x="320" y="56" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-weight="bold">INTRODUCTION</text><text x="320" y="73" text-anchor="middle" fill="#C9A84C" font-size="11">hook → background → claim (thesis)</text>'
  + ''.join(f'<rect x="160" y="{94+i*42}" width="320" height="36" rx="6" fill="{c}"/><text x="320" y="{116+i*42}" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="12"><tspan font-weight="bold">{a}</tspan> {b}</text>' for i, (a, b, c) in enumerate([("Body 1:", "reason 1 + evidence + explanation", "#0047BA"), ("Body 2:", "reason 2 + evidence + explanation", "#0047BA"), ("Counterclaim:", "fair opposing view + concession + rebuttal", "#8a6d1f")])) +
  '<path d="M200 226 h240 l40 50 h-320Z" fill="#DB2F30"/><text x="320" y="248" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-weight="bold">CONCLUSION</text><text x="320" y="265" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="11">restate claim in new words → why it matters → call to action</text>'
  '<text x="560" y="150" font-size="11" fill="#5b6676">transitions</text><text x="560" y="164" font-size="11" fill="#5b6676">link every</text><text x="560" y="178" font-size="11" fill="#5b6676">paragraph</text><path d="M552 110 v110" stroke="#C9A84C" stroke-width="3"/>'
  '<text x="320" y="294" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="#5b6676">Wide at the top: start with the big picture and narrow to your claim. Wide at the bottom: end by widening out to why it matters.</text></svg>')

MODEL = ("<div class='card' style='font-family:Georgia,serif'><h3 style='margin-top:0;font-family:Arial'>Model Essay: Homeschoolers Should Serve Their Community</h3><p class='muted' style='font-family:Arial;margin-top:-6px'>Written by KingCretot Experience as a model. Paragraphs are numbered for discussion.</p>"
         "<p><b>[1]</b> Every Saturday morning, a group of homeschooled kids in our town sorts cans at the food pantry while their parents unload a truck. By noon they have packed boxes for more than fifty families. Learning doesn't happen only at a kitchen table. Homeschool families should make regular community service part of their school year, because it builds real-world skills and strengthens the community that supports them.</p>"
         "<p><b>[2]</b> First, service teaches skills that are hard to learn from a textbook. When students organize a food drive, they plan, budget, write flyers and speak to adults they don't know. The Corporation for National and Community Service has reported that young people who volunteer are more likely to do well in school and to finish their education. A student who has run a project from start to finish has practiced the same skills employers look for.</p>"
         "<p><b>[3]</b> Second, service connects homeschoolers to their neighbors. Homeschooled students learn in many places, from libraries and parks to museums and co-ops, all supported by the community. Giving back is a way to say thank you, and it introduces students to people of different ages and backgrounds. When my family volunteered at a senior center, I learned to play dominoes from a retired Navy cook who had sailed around the world.</p>"
         "<p><b>[4]</b> Some parents argue that their schedules are already full with academics, sports and music. It is true that homeschool days can be busy. However, service can count as learning: measuring and budgeting for a food drive is math, and writing letters to local leaders is English. Even two hours a month can make a difference without taking time away from other subjects.</p>"
         "<p><b>[5]</b> Community service turns students into active citizens who know how to solve real problems. It costs little and gives back a great deal, to the students and to everyone they help. This month, homeschool families should choose one local cause and start serving together.</p></div>")

PACKET = ("<div class='card' style='border-left-color:#0047BA'><h3 style='margin-top:0'>Source Packet: Should Students Still Learn Cursive Handwriting?</h3>"
          "<p><b>Source A (Florida standards):</b> Florida's B.E.S.T. English Language Arts standards, adopted in 2020, require students to learn to write in cursive in grades 3, 4 and 5.</p>"
          "<p><b>Source B (brain research):</b> A 2012 study at Indiana University by Karin James and Laura Engelhardt used brain scans of preschool children. After the children practiced printing letters by hand, letters activated brain areas used in reading more than after they practiced typing or tracing letters. (This study was about printing, not cursive specifically.)</p>"
          "<p><b>Source C (note-taking):</b> A 2014 study by Pam Mueller and Daniel Oppenheimer found that college students who took notes by hand answered conceptual questions about a lecture better than students who typed notes on laptops. Later studies trying to repeat the experiment found smaller or mixed effects.</p>"
          "<p><b>Source D (historical documents):</b> The original Declaration of Independence and Constitution, held at the National Archives, are handwritten in a style of cursive. Many family letters, diaries and records from before the 1990s are also in cursive.</p>"
          "<p><b>Source E (opposing view):</b> Many adults now do most of their writing on keyboards and phones. Critics say time spent on cursive could be used for typing, coding or other skills, and that printed handwriting works just as well for everyday notes.</p></div>")

